---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const page = {
  title: "Laboratory Accreditation vs Quality Certification | SENO",
  h1: "Laboratory Accreditation vs Quality Certification",
  description: "Accreditation, certification, and test-specific competence are different claims. Learn what ISO/IEC 17025 and ISO 9001 cover, and how to verify scope.",
  canonical: "/laboratory-accreditation-vs-quality-certification/",
  author: "SENO Avian Genetic Test",
  date: "November 22, 2026",
};
const articleHtml = `
<p>Two laboratories send buyers an ISO certificate. One document certifies a company's quality management system. The other lists specific tests, methods and a laboratory site under an accreditation schedule. Both may be genuine, yet they answer different questions. The <a href="/avian-testing-laboratory-quality-validation/">laboratory quality and validation guide</a> places both documents inside a wider evidence set.</p>
<p>Buyers often treat the logos as interchangeable. They are not. Accreditation, certification and an internal quality program differ in who assesses the claim, what exactly is assessed and how a buyer can check it. Scope decides whether any of them speaks to the test you are buying.</p>

<h2>Three Different Claims</h2>
<p>Accreditation usually means an independent body has assessed a laboratory against a competence standard for defined activities. Certification usually means an independent body has assessed an organization or system against a specified standard. An internal quality program is the laboratory's own system for planning, checking and improving work; it may be rigorous, but it is not independent assessment.</p>
<p>The words are sometimes used loosely in sales material. Read the supporting document rather than the logo caption. The <a href="/evaluate-avian-dna-testing-laboratory/">laboratory evaluation guide</a> and <a href="/avian-dna-testing-supplier-evaluation/">supplier evaluation guide</a> show how credentials fit into a wider review.</p>

<h2>Accreditation, Certification, and Internal Programs</h2>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table class="data-table">
<thead>
<tr><th>Route</th><th>Unit assessed</th><th>Scope</th><th>Public evidence</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Accreditation</td><td>A laboratory's competence for defined activities</td><td>Named tests, methods, matrices and sites</td><td>Certificate and accreditation scope, often in a directory</td></tr>
<tr><td>Certification</td><td>An organization or management system</td><td>Requirements of a named standard</td><td>Certificate and, where available, a public register entry</td></tr>
<tr><td>Internal quality program</td><td>The laboratory's own processes</td><td>Internally defined procedures and criteria</td><td>SOPs, audits, validation records and QC data on request</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>An accreditation is bounded by its schedule. A certificate is bounded by its stated scope. An internal program is bounded by how honestly it is implemented. None of these documents establishes performance for every species, sample and claim on a laboratory's menu.</p>

<h2>ISO/IEC 17025 and ISO 9001 Ask Different Questions</h2>
<p>ISO/IEC 17025 is a competence standard for testing and calibration laboratories. Its assessment covers both management and technical activities, including method validation, equipment, traceability, sample handling, reporting and quality control. Accreditation to this standard is normally granted for a defined scope, not for every activity a laboratory might offer.</p>
<p>ISO 9001 is a generic quality management standard for organizations. It asks whether the organization plans, controls and improves its processes. Certification to ISO 9001 can be valuable, but it does not independently establish that a particular avian assay is technically valid or that staff can perform it correctly. A company can have a strong management system and an unvalidated new test. For test performance, request <a href="/bird-dna-test-accuracy-validation/">accuracy validation evidence</a> as well.</p>

<h2>The Accreditation Body, the Site, and the Scope</h2>
<p>Ask which accreditation body issued the document, which legal entity and site it covers, and which program was used. A laboratory may operate several sites or brands. Accreditation may apply to one location, one department or one test category. A site not named in the schedule should not be presented as covered by it.</p>
<p>Verification should begin with the issuing body's directory or published scope, not a screenshot attached to a quote. If the directory is unavailable, ask the laboratory for a current certificate and the complete schedule, then confirm the issuing body's identity independently.</p>

<h2>Reading a Scope Document</h2>
<p>A scope document usually identifies the accredited activity, the method or standard, the sample or matrix, the site and any limitations. Read the entry for your test rather than the cover page. Look for the target, platform or method name, the sample type and whether the activity is described as screening, detection, identification or another defined purpose.</p>
<p>An entry that says "veterinary diagnostic testing" without naming the test may still have detail in an annex or method list. Ask for the part that covers your service. If the laboratory offers a new avian panel that is absent from the schedule, the panel may be valid and useful, but it is not evidenced by that accreditation scope.</p>

<h2>Expiry, Suspension, and Change of Scope</h2>
<p>Certificates and scopes have validity dates and revision histories. A copy with no date is weak evidence. Check whether the document is current, whether the scope has changed since the copy was issued and whether the status is active. Suspension or withdrawal may appear in the issuing body's directory before it appears in sales material.</p>
<p>Accreditation is not permanent proof. Methods change, sites move and schedules are revised. Ask whether a change affects the specific tests you buy, and keep the dated evidence used for the decision. A supplier should be able to explain a scope revision without treating the question as an accusation.</p>

<h2>Avian-Specific Accreditation and Supplier Claims</h2>
<p>Veterinary diagnostic accreditation programs add a sector-specific route. The American Association of Veterinary Laboratory Diagnosticians (AAVLD) describes an accreditation program for veterinary diagnostic laboratories. It can be one useful reference point for a buyer assessing an avian laboratory. This page uses AAVLD only as an example; it makes no claim that SENO or any other laboratory named here holds that accreditation.</p>
<p>Treat every sector credential as scope-bound and time-bound. A program name does not tell you which test, species, sample or site is covered. Ask for the document and verify it with the issuing organization.</p>
<div class="table-wrap">
<table class="data-table">
<thead>
<tr><th>Claim</th><th>Document to request</th><th>How to verify</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>We are an accredited laboratory</td><td>Current accreditation certificate and full scope</td><td>Check the issuing body's directory, legal entity and site</td></tr>
<tr><td>Our avian test is accredited</td><td>Scope entry naming the test, method and sample</td><td>Match the entry to the service you are buying</td></tr>
<tr><td>We are ISO 9001 certified</td><td>Certificate with scope, entity, site and validity</td><td>Confirm the issuer and certification status</td></tr>
<tr><td>We are AAVLD accredited</td><td>Current listing or certificate and covered activities</td><td>Check with AAVLD or its published directory</td></tr>
<tr><td>All our results are quality assured</td><td>Validation, control, proficiency-testing and review records</td><td>Request the evidence; a logo does not prove it</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<h2>Scenario: Two ISO Certificates</h2>
<p>A buyer requests credentials from two laboratories. Laboratory A sends an ISO 9001 certificate naming the company and its management system. Laboratory B sends an ISO/IEC 17025 accreditation schedule with a narrow test scope. The buyer wants to know which laboratory is better qualified.</p>
<p>The documents answer different questions. Laboratory A's certificate can support confidence that a quality management system has been certified against ISO 9001. It does not, by itself, show that the specific avian test was validated or that the staff and method are competent. The buyer should still request the validation record, control policy, sample-identification procedure and report template.</p>
<p>Laboratory B's accreditation schedule can support competence for the activities inside its scope. The buyer should check whether the named test, method, sample type and site match the intended work. A narrow scope is not automatically a weakness; it may show a laboratory defines its claims carefully. It does mean that work outside the scope is not covered by that accreditation.</p>
<p>If the test the buyer needs is inside Laboratory B's schedule, the accreditation is relevant evidence, but it is not the whole decision. Ask for the same operational documents requested from Laboratory A. If the test is outside the schedule, Laboratory B may still be capable, yet its accreditation does not establish that point.</p>
<p>The better buy depends on the service, the evidence available for that service and the consequences of a wrong result. The buyer should not rank the laboratories by the prestige of the logo. The useful next step is to place both certificates beside their scopes, compare them with the requested test, and then ask each laboratory for the missing technical evidence.</p>

<h2>What we would do</h2>
<p>We would ask for the current certificate, complete scope and issuing-body verification before interpreting a credential. We would match the legal entity, site, test, method and sample to the work being purchased, then request validation and QC records for that service. The exception is a claim that affects no decision, such as a general company certification; we would note it but not treat it as test evidence. We would keep the verified copy on file.</p>

<h2>Questions buyers ask</h2>
<h3>Is ISO/IEC 17025 accreditation better than ISO 9001 certification?</h3>
<p>They answer different questions. ISO/IEC 17025 accreditation addresses laboratory competence for a defined scope. ISO 9001 certification addresses an organization's quality management system. One is not a substitute for the other, and neither replaces test-specific validation.</p>
<h3>Does accreditation cover every test the laboratory offers?</h3>
<p>No. Accreditation is limited to the activities, methods, sites and sample types listed in the scope. A laboratory may offer additional tests outside that scope. Ask for the schedule and match the exact service rather than assuming the logo covers the menu.</p>
<h3>How can I tell whether an ISO certificate is real?</h3>
<p>Check the issuing body, certificate number, legal entity, site, standard, scope and validity dates. Verify through the issuer's directory or published register where possible. A screenshot or a logo without a traceable certificate and scope is not enough.</p>
<h3>What if the laboratory is accredited for a related test but not mine?</h3>
<p>The related accreditation can show a relevant quality system, but it does not establish competence for the specific test. Ask for validation evidence for your test and sample, and treat the accreditation claim as limited to its documented scope.</p>
<h3>Does AAVLD accreditation guarantee avian test quality?</h3>
<p>No credential guarantees every result. AAVLD accreditation is an example of a sector-specific program that should be checked for current status and covered activities. A buyer still needs test-specific validation, controls and reporting evidence for the service being purchased.</p>

<h2>Sources</h2>
<ol>
<li><a href="https://www.iso.org/ISO-IEC-17025-testing-and-calibration-laboratories.html">ISO/IEC 17025: testing and calibration laboratories</a>, International Organization for Standardization.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.iso.org/standard/66912.html">ISO/IEC 17025:2017 standard overview</a>, International Organization for Standardization.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.iso.org/quality-management/">ISO quality management</a>, International Organization for Standardization.</li>
<li><a href="https://www.aavld.org/accreditation-requirements">Accreditation requirements</a>, American Association of Veterinary Laboratory Diagnosticians.</li>
</ol>
`;
